    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d798">

                <pb facs="1074" n="964" sameAs="frus:frus1928v03/pg964"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">367.1164 Brusa School Trial/1: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-01-22T20:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 22,
                                1928—8 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received January 22—6
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>5. In view of Turkish law prohibiting religious propaganda in schools, Turkish
                    Government has sent representatives to investigate alleged conversion to
                    Christianity of four girl students in American school at Brusa whose diaries
                    were stolen and turned over to Turkish authorities. Goodsell<anchor n="46"
                        xml:id="d798-f1"/> states American school has been conforming <pb
                        facs="1075" n="965" xml:id="pg965"/> scrupulously antipropaganda law;
                    therefore, American board welcomes investigation and believes incident will be
                    settled satisfactorily. I shall report result.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d798-f1" n="46" xml:id="d798-f1-content">
                <p>Fred Field Goodsell, field secretary of the Turkish Mission of the American Board
                    of Commissioners for Foreign Missions.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
